22 0 obj
<< /A 229 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating the thyroid hormone feedback loop. The thyroid gland produces T4 and T3 hormones, which circulate in the bloodstream. The brain releases TRH, stimulating TSH production, influencing thyroid activity. T4 is converted to T3 by deiodinases in various tissues, impacting organs like the brain, muscles, liver, and kidneys, categorized as slow and fast processes.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 14 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 230 0 R /Alt <> /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 231 0 R /Alt (The image shows two screenshots from the p-THYROSIM iOS app. The left screenshot displays the app's homepage with a diagram of the thyroid hormone regulation system, a button to read more about the app, references, and acknowledgments. The right screenshot features the \215Run 2 Dosing Input\216 screen, which includes instructions on choosing T3 and T4 input doses via oral, intravenous bolus, or infusion methods, and a button to start the simulation.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 16 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 232 0 R /Alt (Simulation interface for thyroid conditions features two main panels. Left panel allows adjustments to simulation conditions like T3/T4 secretion and absorption rates, gender, height, and weight. Right panel displays graphs for Free T4, Free T3, and TSH levels over time with a toggle for overlaying previous data runs. Reset options are available for adjustments and data visualization.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 17 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /A 233 0 R /Alt (Two screenshots display dosing simulations with graphs for Free T4, Free T3, and TSH levels. The left image shows Free T4 rising gradually, Free T3 stable, and TSH oscillating. The right image shows similar trends, but with increased oscillations in Free T3 and TSH. Normal ranges are shaded in yellow.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 18 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /A 234 0 R /Alt (Graph showing thyroid hormone levels over time in three panels. The top panel \(Free T4\) shows three runs: Run 1 \(red\) remains stable, Run 2 \(purple\) and Run 3 \(blue\) show upward trends. The middle panel \(Free T3\) displays stable Run 1 and Run 2, while Run 3 fluctuates significantly. The bottom panel \(TSH\) shows all runs with pronounced peaks and troughs. Normal ranges are highlighted in yellow.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 19 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
28 0 obj
<< /A 235 0 R /Alt (Graphs showing predicted hormone responses over 240 days. The top graph displays FT4 levels peaking initially, then stabilizing. The middle graph shows FT3 levels with a similar pattern. The bottom graph illustrates TSH levels peaking early, then decreasing. Dose regimes: 150 micrograms LT4 with 0 micrograms LT3, 75 micrograms LT4 with 0 micrograms LT3, and 100 micrograms LT4 with 7.5 micrograms LT3.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 20 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
